\begin{abstract}
For a nonzero function $f$ on a finite cyclic group $\Zn_n$ the Donoho--Stark uncertainty
principle asserts the multiplicative bound $|\supp f|\cdot|\supp\hat f|\ge n$. When $n=p$ is
prime, a strictly stronger \emph{additive} bound $|\supp f|+|\supp\hat f|\ge p+1$ is available.
This paper develops the additive principle systematically: we delimit it from above and below,
identify it with a classical determinantal statement, reduce it to a finite combinatorial
criterion, and prove it outright in five explicit regimes. Specifically: (i) the additive bound
implies the multiplicative one, while no arithmetic manipulation of the multiplicative bound can
yield the additive one --- for every $p\ge5$ there exist admissible support cardinalities with
$ab\ge p$ and $a+b<p+1$, and the multiplicative bound in isolation yields only the square-root
estimate $a+b\ge 2\sqrt n$; (ii) primality is indispensable --- on $\Zn_4$ the indicator of the
subgroup $\{0,2\}$ attains the multiplicative bound with equality while violating the additive
one; (iii) the additive bound is sharp at both endpoints, attained by Dirac deltas $(1+p)$ and
by characters $(p+1)$; (iv) for every modulus the additive bound is \emph{equivalent} to the
nonsingularity of all square minors of the Fourier matrix $(\omega^{st})_{s,t}$ (Chebotarev's
property), and equivalent in turn to a purely combinatorial statement about parity-weighted
fibres of the exponent map $\sigma\mapsto\sum_j s_{\sigma(j)}t_j$ on the symmetric group;
(v) the additive bound holds whenever $|\supp f|\le3$, whenever $|\supp\hat f|\le3$, whenever
$|\supp f|\ge p-3$, and whenever either the support or the spectrum is an arithmetic
progression. As an application we derive a deterministic, universal sparse-recovery guarantee:
any $k$-sparse function on $\Zp$ is uniquely determined by its Fourier coefficients on
\emph{any} set of $2k$ frequencies.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} uncertainty principle, discrete Fourier transform, cyclic group of
prime order, Chebotarev's theorem, roots of unity, sparse recovery, compressed sensing,
Vandermonde determinant.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Setting and notation}

Fix $n\ge1$, let $\Zn_n$ be the integers modulo $n$, put $\omega=e^{2\pi i/n}$, and let
$\chi(a)=\omega^{\bar a}$ where $\bar a\in\{0,\dots,n-1\}$ is the canonical representative of
$a$. Then $\chi:(\Zn_n,+)\to(\mathbb{C}^\times,\cdot)$ is a group homomorphism, injective when
$n$ is prime; we write $\omega^a$ for $\chi(a)$.

For $f:\Zn_n\to\mathbb{C}$ the unnormalised discrete Fourier transform is
\[
\hat f(k)=\sum_{x\in\Zn_n}\omega^{-kx}f(x),\qquad k\in\Zn_n,
\]
and $\supp f=\{x: f(x)\ne0\}$. Fourier inversion takes the reflection form
\begin{equation}\label{eq:refl}
\hat{\hat f}(k)=n\,f(-k),
\end{equation}
whence $|\supp\hat{\hat f}|=|\supp f|$ and $f\ne0\Rightarrow\hat f\ne0$. Identity
\eqref{eq:refl} is the source of all duality statements below.

\subsection{The two uncertainty inequalities}

\begin{theorem}[Product bound; Donoho--Stark]
For every $n\ge1$ and every nonzero $f:\Zn_n\to\mathbb{C}$,
$|\supp f|\cdot|\supp\hat f|\ge n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Additive bound]
For every prime $p$ and every nonzero $f:\Zp\to\mathbb{C}$,
$|\supp f|+|\supp\hat f|\ge p+1$.
\end{theorem}

We write $\mathrm{SU}(n)$ for the assertion ``$|\supp f|+|\supp\hat f|\ge n+1$ for all
$f\ne0$'' and $\mathrm{CP}(n)$ for ``every square submatrix of $(\omega^{st})_{s,t\in\Zn_n}$ is
nonsingular''. Both make sense for arbitrary moduli; Section~\ref{sec:det} proves that they are
equivalent for every modulus.

\section{Separating the two bounds}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:sum-implies-prod}
Let $a,b\ge1$ be integers with $a+b\ge p+1$. Then $ab\ge p$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
$(a-1)(b-1)\ge0$ gives $ab\ge a+b-1\ge p$.
\end{proof}

Applied with $a=|\supp f|$, $b=|\supp\hat f|$, this shows that $\mathrm{SU}(p)$ implies the
Donoho--Stark bound for $\Zp$.

\begin{theorem}[No arithmetic implication]\label{thm:no-implication}
For every $p\ge5$ there exist positive integers $a,b$ with $p\le ab$ and $a+b<p+1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Take $a=2$, $b=\lfloor(p+1)/2\rfloor$. Then $2b\ge p$ (equal to $p+1$ for $p$ odd and to $p$ for
$p$ even), so $ab\ge p$; and $a+b=2+\lfloor(p+1)/2\rfloor<p+1$ precisely because
$\lfloor(p+1)/2\rfloor<p-1$ for $p\ge5$.
\end{proof}

Thus the additive bound is unobtainable from the multiplicative one by any inequality
manipulation: the cardinality pair $(2,\lfloor(p+1)/2\rfloor)$ is admissible for the product
bound and inadmissible for the additive one. The same holds for the balanced profile
$a=b=\lceil\sqrt p\rceil$.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:amgm}
For all integers $x,y\ge0$ one has $4xy\le(x+y)^2$; hence for every $n\ge1$ and nonzero $f$ on
$\Zn_n$,
\[
\big(|\supp f|+|\supp\hat f|\big)^2\ge4n,\qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad
|\supp f|+|\supp\hat f|\ge2\sqrt n .
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
$(x+y)^2-4xy=(x-y)^2\ge0$; combine with the product bound.
\end{proof}

The gap between $2\sqrt p$ and $p+1$ quantifies the strength of the additive principle: the
multiplicative bound recovers only the square root of the additive truth.

\section{Boundaries: primality and sharpness}

\begin{theorem}[Primality is essential]\label{thm:z4}
Let $f:\Zn_4\to\mathbb{C}$ be the indicator of the subgroup $\{0,2\}$. Then $f\ne0$ and
\[
|\supp f|\cdot|\supp\hat f|=4,\qquad |\supp f|+|\supp\hat f|=4<5 .
\]
The additive bound therefore fails for the modulus $4$, while the multiplicative bound holds
with equality.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
With $\omega=i$ one has $\omega^{-2}=-1$, so $\hat f(k)=1+\omega^{-2k}=1+(-1)^k$. Hence
$\hat f(0)=\hat f(2)=2$ and $\hat f(1)=\hat f(3)=0$, so $\supp\hat f=\{0,2\}=\supp f$, each of
cardinality $2$.
\end{proof}

The mechanism is structural: the indicator of a subgroup $H\le G$ transforms into $|H|$ times
the indicator of the annihilator $H^{\perp}$, and $|H|\cdot|H^{\perp}|=|G|$, so subgroup
indicators always attain the Donoho--Stark bound. A group of prime order has no proper
nontrivial subgroup, which removes the extremal family entirely.

\begin{theorem}[Deltas]\label{thm:delta}
Let $p$ be prime and $\delta_a$ the indicator of $\{a\}\subseteq\Zp$. Then
$\hat{\delta_a}(k)=\omega^{-ka}\ne0$ for all $k$, hence
$|\supp\delta_a|+|\supp\hat{\delta_a}|=1+p$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Only $x=a$ contributes to the defining sum, and a root of unity is never zero.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Full character sum]\label{lem:charsum}
For $p\ge2$, $\sum_{y\in\Zp}\omega^{y}=0$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Re-indexing by canonical representatives gives the geometric sum
$\sum_{m=0}^{p-1}\omega^m=(\omega^p-1)/(\omega-1)=0$, since $\omega^p=1$ and $\omega\ne1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Characters]\label{thm:char}
Let $p$ be prime, $b\in\Zp$, and $\chi_b(x)=\omega^{bx}$. Then $\hat{\chi_b}(k)=p$ if $k=b$ and
$0$ otherwise; hence $|\supp\chi_b|+|\supp\hat{\chi_b}|=p+1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\hat{\chi_b}(k)=\sum_x\omega^{(b-k)x}$. For $k=b$ every term is $1$. For $k\ne b$ the element
$b-k$ is invertible modulo the prime $p$, so $x\mapsto(b-k)x$ permutes $\Zp$ and the sum equals
$\sum_y\omega^y=0$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:charsum}.
\end{proof}

\section{The determinantal identity}\label{sec:det}

\begin{definition}
For $n\ge1$ let $\mathrm{CP}(n)$ be the statement: for every $m\ge0$ and all injective
$S,T:\{1,\dots,m\}\to\Zn_n$ the matrix $M_{S,T}=(\omega^{S_jT_k})_{j,k=1}^m$ is nonsingular.
\end{definition}

Chebotarev's theorem is the assertion $\mathrm{CP}(p)$ for $p$ prime.

\begin{theorem}[Uncertainty $=$ nonsingularity]\label{thm:equiv}
For every modulus $n\ge1$, $\mathrm{CP}(n)\iff\mathrm{SU}(n)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Rightarrow$) Let $f\ne0$ violate the bound, $A=\supp f$ and $Z=\{k:\hat f(k)=0\}$. Since
$|Z|=n-|\supp\hat f|$, the violation reads $|A|\le|Z|$. Choose $Z'\subseteq Z$ with $|Z'|=|A|$,
enumerate $A$ as $T_1,\dots,T_m$ and $Z'$ as $z_1,\dots,z_m$, and put $S_j=-z_j$. The vector
$v_k=f(T_k)$ is nonzero and
\[
\sum_k\omega^{S_jT_k}v_k=\sum_{x\in A}\omega^{-z_jx}f(x)=\hat f(z_j)=0\quad\text{for all }j,
\]
so $M_{S,T}$ is singular.

($\Leftarrow$) If $M_{S,T}v=0$ with $v\ne0$, define $f=\sum_kv_k\delta_{T_k}$; then
$|\supp f|\le m$, and $\hat f(-S_j)=\sum_k\omega^{S_jT_k}v_k=0$ for $m$ distinct frequencies
$-S_j$, so $|\supp\hat f|\le n-m$ and $|\supp f|+|\supp\hat f|\le n$, contradicting
$\mathrm{SU}(n)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
$\mathrm{CP}(4)$ is false: the minor of the Fourier matrix of $\Zn_4$ on rows $\{0,2\}$ and
columns $\{0,2\}$ is the all-ones $2\times2$ matrix.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proposition}[Local reduction]\label{prop:local}
Let $f\ne0$ on $\Zn_n$ and $m=|\supp f|$. If every $m\times m$ minor of the Fourier matrix of
$\Zn_n$ is nonsingular, then $|\supp f|+|\supp\hat f|\ge n+1$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Arithmetic-progression minors]\label{thm:vandermonde}
Let $p$ be prime, $a,d\in\Zp$ with $d\ne0$, and $T:\{1,\dots,m\}\to\Zp$ injective. Then the
minor with rows indexed by the progression $a,a+d,\dots,a+(m-1)d$, namely
$M_{j,k}=\omega^{(a+(j-1)d)T_k}$, is nonsingular. The same holds with rows and columns
exchanged.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Set $z_k=\omega^{dT_k}$ and $c_k=\omega^{aT_k}$, so $M_{j,k}=c_kz_k^{\,j-1}$, i.e.
$M=V(z)^{\mathsf T}\diag(c)$ with $V(z)$ the Vandermonde matrix of the nodes $z_k$. Since $p$ is
prime and $d\ne0$, the map $t\mapsto\omega^{dt}$ is injective, so the $z_k$ are pairwise
distinct and $\det V(z)=\prod_{j<k}(z_k-z_j)\ne0$; also $\det\diag(c)=\prod_kc_k\ne0$. The
transposed statement follows by taking determinants of transposes.
\end{proof}

\section{Proved regimes}

Throughout, $p$ is prime and $f:\Zp\to\mathbb{C}$ is nonzero.

\begin{theorem}[No vanishing on a short progression]\label{thm:window}
Let $a,d\in\Zp$ with $d\ne0$. Then $\hat f(a+jd)\ne0$ for some $j<|\supp f|$; equivalently,
$\hat f$ cannot vanish identically on an arithmetic progression of length $|\supp f|$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Write $A=\supp f$, $z_x=\omega^{-dx}$, $c_x=\omega^{-ax}f(x)$. Since $p$ is prime and $d\ne0$,
the $z_x$ ($x\in A$) are pairwise distinct, and
\[
\hat f(a+jd)=\sum_{x\in A}\omega^{-(a+jd)x}f(x)=\sum_{x\in A}c_x z_x^{\,j}.
\]
Assume this vanishes for $j=0,\dots,|A|-1$. Fix $x_0\in A$ and put
$L(X)=\prod_{x\in A\setminus\{x_0\}}(X-z_x)$, of degree $|A|-1$. Taking the linear combination
of the vanishing sums given by the monomial coefficients of $L$ yields
$\sum_{x\in A}c_xL(z_x)=0$, i.e. $c_{x_0}L(z_{x_0})=0$; but $L(z_{x_0})\ne0$ by distinctness, so
$f(x_0)=0$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Supports inside a progression]\label{thm:AP}
If $\supp f\subseteq\{a+jd: 0\le j<m\}$ with $d\ne0$ and $m\le p$, then
$|\supp\hat f|\ge p+1-m$. In particular, if $\supp f$ equals such a progression then
$|\supp f|+|\supp\hat f|\ge p+1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $P(X)=\sum_{j=0}^{m-1}f(a+jd)X^j$, a nonzero polynomial of degree $\le m-1$. Splitting
$-k(a+jd)=-ka+j(-kd)$ gives $\hat f(k)=\omega^{-ka}P(\omega^{-kd})$. Since $\omega^{-ka}\ne0$,
each zero $k$ of $\hat f$ yields a root $\omega^{-kd}$ of $P$, and $k\mapsto\omega^{-kd}$ is
injective. A nonzero polynomial of degree $\le m-1$ has at most $m-1$ roots, so $\hat f$ has at
most $m-1$ zeros.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Dual version]
If $\supp\hat f$ is an arithmetic progression with nonzero common difference and length $\le p$,
then $|\supp f|+|\supp\hat f|\ge p+1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:AP} to $g=\hat f$ and use \eqref{eq:refl}:
$|\supp\hat g|=|\supp f|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[At most two nonzero values]\label{thm:two}
If $|\supp f|\le2$ then $|\supp f|+|\supp\hat f|\ge p+1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Otherwise, with $Z$ the zero set of $\hat f$, we get $|\supp f|\le|Z|$. If $|\supp f|=1$, pick
$a\in Z$: then $\hat f$ vanishes on the length-$1$ progression $\{a\}$, contradicting
Theorem~\ref{thm:window} with $d=1$. If $|\supp f|=2$, pick distinct $a,b\in Z$: then $\hat f$
vanishes on the length-$2$ progression $\{a,a+(b-a)\}$ with $b-a\ne0$, again a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[$3\times3$ Chebotarev]\label{thm:three}
For distinct $S_1,S_2,S_3$ and distinct $T_1,T_2,T_3$ in $\Zp$ the matrix
$(\omega^{S_jT_k})_{j,k=1}^3$ is nonsingular.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The Leibniz expansion writes the determinant as the six-term signed sum
$\omega^{e_1}+\omega^{e_2}+\omega^{e_3}-\omega^{f_1}-\omega^{f_2}-\omega^{f_3}$ of $p$-th roots
of unity. Distinctness of rows and columns forces one negative exponent, say $f_1$, to differ
from all three positive ones, since the pairwise differences factor as products
$(S_j-S_k)(T_l-T_m)$ of nonzero elements of the field $\Zp$. The coefficient function of the
sum then sums to $0$ over $\Zp$ and is nonzero at $f_1$, so Theorem~\ref{thm:criterion-basic}
applies.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[At most three nonzero values, and duals]\label{thm:threevals}
Each of the following implies $|\supp f|+|\supp\hat f|\ge p+1$:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item $|\supp f|\le3$;
\item $|\supp\hat f|\le3$;
\item $|\supp f|\ge p-3$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
(i) For $|\supp f|\le2$ use Theorem~\ref{thm:two}; for $|\supp f|=3$ combine
Proposition~\ref{prop:local} with Theorem~\ref{thm:three}. (ii) Apply (i) to $\hat f$ and use
\eqref{eq:refl}. (iii) If $|\supp\hat f|\le3$ apply (ii); otherwise $|\supp\hat f|\ge4$ and
$|\supp f|+|\supp\hat f|\ge(p-3)+4=p+1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Known regimes]\label{thm:master}
Let $p$ be prime and $f\ne0$ on $\Zp$. If any one of the following holds, then
$|\supp f|+|\supp\hat f|\ge p+1$:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
\item $|\supp f|\le3$;
\item $|\supp\hat f|\le3$;
\item $|\supp f|\ge p-3$;
\item $\supp f$ is an arithmetic progression with nonzero common difference;
\item $\supp\hat f$ is an arithmetic progression with nonzero common difference.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\section{From analysis to combinatorics}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:indep}
Let $p$ be prime and $c:\Zp\to\mathbb{Q}$. If $\sum_{r\in\Zp}c_r\omega^r=0$, then $c$ is
constant.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The minimal polynomial of $\omega$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ is $1+X+\dots+X^{p-1}$, so a rational
polynomial of degree $<p$ vanishing at $\omega$ is a rational multiple of $1+X+\dots+X^{p-1}$;
that is exactly the statement that all coefficients coincide.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Vanishing criterion]\label{thm:criterion-basic}
Let $c:\Zp\to\mathbb{Q}$ satisfy $\sum_rc_r=0$. If $c_{r_0}\ne0$ for some $r_0$, then
$\sum_rc_r\omega^r\ne0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If the sum vanished, Lemma~\ref{lem:indep} would give $c_r=c_{r_0}$ for all $r$, whence
$0=\sum_rc_r=p\,c_{r_0}$ and $c_{r_0}=0$.
\end{proof}

Fix $n\ge1$ and injective $S,T:\{1,\dots,n\}\to\Zp$. For $\sigma\in S_n$ define the
\emph{exponent} $E_\sigma=\sum_{j=1}^nS_{\sigma(j)}T_j\in\Zp$, and for $r\in\Zp$ the
\emph{parity-weighted multiplicity}
\[
c_r=\sum_{\sigma:\,E_\sigma=r}\sgn(\sigma)
 =\#\{\text{even }\sigma: E_\sigma=r\}-\#\{\text{odd }\sigma: E_\sigma=r\}\in\Zn .
\]

\begin{lemma}[Leibniz expansion]\label{lem:leibniz}
$\det(\omega^{S_jT_k})_{j,k}=\sum_{r\in\Zp}c_r\omega^r$, and for $n\ge2$,
$\sum_rc_r=\sum_{\sigma\in S_n}\sgn(\sigma)=0$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The Leibniz formula and the character property give
$\det=\sum_\sigma\sgn(\sigma)\prod_j\omega^{S_{\sigma(j)}T_j}=\sum_\sigma\sgn(\sigma)
\omega^{E_\sigma}$; group by exponent. The second identity is the standard fact that $S_n$ has
equally many even and odd elements for $n\ge2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Combinatorial criterion for Chebotarev]\label{thm:criterion}
Let $n\ge2$. Then $\det(\omega^{S_jT_k})_{j,k}\ne0$ if and only if $c_r\ne0$ for some
$r\in\Zp$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Combine Lemmas~\ref{lem:leibniz} and Theorem~\ref{thm:criterion-basic} for one direction; the
other is immediate from Lemma~\ref{lem:leibniz}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Odd fibre]\label{cor:odd}
If some residue $r$ has an odd number of preimages under $\sigma\mapsto E_\sigma$, then
$c_r\ne0$ and the minor is nonsingular.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Unique realisation]\label{cor:unique}
If some $\sigma_0$ realises its exponent uniquely, then $c_{E_{\sigma_0}}=\sgn(\sigma_0)=\pm1$
and the minor is nonsingular.
\end{corollary}

Chebotarev's theorem --- equivalently, by Theorem~\ref{thm:equiv}, the additive uncertainty
principle --- is therefore exactly the finite statement that for distinct rows and distinct
columns the map $\sigma\mapsto E_\sigma$ never distributes the permutations of $\{1,\dots,n\}$
in perfect parity balance across all residues modulo $p$.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Exact minor test by parity-weighted counting.}
Given a prime $p$ and injective $S,T\in\Zp^n$ with $n\ge2$: for each $\sigma\in S_n$ compute
$e=\sum_jS_{\sigma(j)}T_j \bmod p$ and add $\sgn(\sigma)$ to a counter $c[e]$; report
nonsingularity iff some counter is nonzero. Complexity $O(n!\,n)$ time, $O(p)$ space. The test
uses integers only, so it is free of floating-point error.

\paragraph{Exhaustive verification at fixed size.}
Iterate the previous test over all $\binom{p}{n}^2$ pairs of row/column sets; complexity
$O(\binom pn^2 n!\,n)$. For $p\in\{5,7,11,13\}$ and $n\le4$ this exact integer computation
finds every minor nonsingular (e.g.\ all $511{,}225$ minors of size $4$ for $p=13$) --- a finite
check rather than a proof for general $p$. The same computation measures the reach of
Corollary~\ref{cor:unique}: for $p=11$, $n=4$ about $89\%$ of minors have a singleton fibre, for
$p=13$, $n=4$ about $91\%$, while for $p=7$, $n=4$ \emph{none} does. By contrast, in every
configuration examined some fibre has odd cardinality, so Corollary~\ref{cor:odd} applies
throughout the computed range; the minimum parity gap $\max_r|c_r|$ over all $108{,}900$ minors
of size $4$ for $p=11$ is $2$.

\paragraph{Deterministic sparse recovery.}
Given $2k$ Fourier samples of a $k$-sparse $f$, enumerate candidate supports $A$ of size $k$,
solve the overdetermined least-squares system $(\omega^{-sx})_{s\in\Omega,x\in A}v=\hat
f|_\Omega$, and return the candidate of least residual. The cost is
$O(\binom pk k^2|\Omega|)$; this brute force is a uniqueness demonstration, with Prony's method
or $\ell^1$ minimisation as the practical replacements, both correct here precisely because the
relevant minors are nonsingular.

\section{Application: deterministic sparse recovery}

\begin{theorem}[Universal $2k$-sample uniqueness]\label{thm:recovery}
Let $p$ be prime and $k\ge1$ with $2k\le p$, and suppose the additive uncertainty principle
holds for every function with at most $2k$ nonzero values (unconditional for $k\le1$ by
Theorem~\ref{thm:threevals}; in general it is exactly the nonsingularity of the Fourier minors
of size $\le2k$). Let $\Omega\subseteq\Zp$ be \emph{any} set of $2k$ frequencies. If $f$ and $g$
are $k$-sparse and $\hat f|_\Omega=\hat g|_\Omega$, then $f=g$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Put $h=f-g$; then $|\supp h|\le2k$ and $\hat h$ vanishes on $\Omega$, so
$|\supp\hat h|\le p-2k$. If $h\ne0$, the additive bound gives
$|\supp h|\ge(p+1)-(p-2k)=2k+1$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

Equivalently: the $2k\times2k$ submatrix of the Fourier matrix on rows $\Omega$ and any $2k$
columns is nonsingular, so the partial Fourier measurement operator is injective on
$2k$-sparse vectors --- the spark of the partial Fourier matrix of $\Zp$ is maximal, equal to
$|\Omega|+1$. Three features distinguish this from generic compressed-sensing guarantees: it is
deterministic (no randomness, no failure probability, no restricted-isometry hypothesis);
universal in the sampling pattern (every set of $2k$ frequencies works); and optimal in the
sample count, since with $2k-1$ frequencies the constraints have rank at most $2k-1$ on a
$2k$-dimensional space of admissible vectors, producing a nonzero $2k$-sparse ambiguity. On
$\Zn_4$, by contrast, sampling the frequencies $\{1,3\}$ conveys no information at all about the
subgroup indicator of $\{0,2\}$.

\section{Discussion}

Primality enters three times. First, the extremal family for Donoho--Stark consists of cosets
of subgroups (Theorem~\ref{thm:z4}), which prime order eliminates. Second, $\Zp$ is a field, so
$x\mapsto dx$ is a bijection for $d\ne0$ (Lemma~\ref{lem:charsum},
Theorem~\ref{thm:vandermonde}, Theorem~\ref{thm:window}) and differences of distinct elements
are invertible (Theorem~\ref{thm:three}). Third --- and deepest --- the $p$-th cyclotomic
polynomial is $1+X+\dots+X^{p-1}$, so the only rational relation among
$1,\omega,\dots,\omega^{p-1}$ is the obvious one (Lemma~\ref{lem:indep}); for composite $n$
there are further relations, e.g.\ $1+i^2=0$ in $\Zn_4$, and these are exactly what manufacture
singular minors.

The parity criterion reduces everything to the fibre structure of $\sigma\mapsto E_\sigma$. All
currently available sufficient conditions concern a single fibre in isolation, whereas for
$n\ge4$ fibres can be large and even in size; and yet, in every configuration examined, at least
one fibre of odd cardinality survives, which alone would settle the theorem. Proving that an odd
fibre always exists, or otherwise finding an invariant that distinguishes even from odd
permutations \emph{inside} a fibre, is the missing step.

\section{Future directions}

The remaining open case is $4\le|\supp f|\le p-4$ with unstructured support and unstructured
spectrum.

\begin{conjecture}[Parity-gap conjecture]
For all $n\ge2$ and all injective $S,T:\{1,\dots,n\}\to\Zp$, some parity-weighted multiplicity
$c_r$ is nonzero; in fact $\max_r|c_r|\ge1$ is attained at a residue of the form
$\sum_jS_{\sigma(j)}T_j$ for a permutation $\sigma$ of minimal Coxeter length among those
realising that exponent.
\end{conjecture}

The key insight is that Theorem~\ref{thm:criterion} reduces an analytic non-vanishing statement
about roots of unity to a counting statement about $S_n$ acting on $\Zp$, so the whole
difficulty is concentrated in the fibres of $\sigma\mapsto\sum_jS_{\sigma(j)}T_j$ --- and the
numerical data show the fibres are never parity-balanced. Corollary~\ref{cor:unique} already
discharges every configuration in which one fibre is a singleton, covering $n\le3$ completely;
the length statistic is the natural candidate for the missing invariant, and is finitely
checkable for $n=4$.

\begin{conjecture}[$(1-\omega)$-adic valuation conjecture]
Write $M(u)=\det\big((1+u)^{s_jt_k}\big)\in\Zn[u]$. Then the order of vanishing of $M$ at $u=0$
equals $n(n-1)/2$ and the leading coefficient is
\[
\frac{\prod_{j<k}(s_k-s_j)(t_k-t_j)}{\prod_{j<k}(k-j)},
\]
an integer prime to $p$; consequently every Fourier minor is nonsingular.
\end{conjecture}

The key insight is that the exponent-$u$ filtration of $\Zn[u]$ refines the vanishing of the
minor modulo the prime $(1-\omega)$ of $\Zn[\omega]$, converting Chebotarev's theorem into an
identity between a generalised Vandermonde determinant and a Weyl-type dimension form.

Beyond these: quantitative versions for $\varepsilon$-concentrated rather than exactly supported
signals; the analogous questions on $\Zn_{p^2}$ and on products of distinct primes, where the
subgroup obstruction returns in a controlled way; and algorithmic exploitation of maximal spark,
in particular provably correct Prony-type reconstruction with worst-case guarantees.

\section{Conclusion}

The additive uncertainty principle $|\supp f|+|\supp\hat f|\ge p+1$ on $\Zp$ is strictly
stronger than the classical product bound, provably unreachable from it by arithmetic, and
provably false without primality. It is sharp exactly at the delta and character endpoints; it
is equivalent, for every modulus, to the nonsingularity of all minors of the Fourier matrix;
and that is in turn equivalent to a finite statement about parity-weighted fibres of an exponent
map on the symmetric group. Along this chain the principle is established for supports or
spectra of size at most three, for very large supports, and for arithmetic-progression supports
or spectra, and it yields a deterministic, sampling-pattern-universal sparse-recovery guarantee
at the optimal rate of $2k$ measurements. What remains is a single, sharply delineated
combinatorial question about how permutations distribute across residues.

